From 67dd6c151aff7b176da621bb5056e3e3327e913c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:48:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 04/16] [bigint] divide Nats as nonnegative integers

Divisions of `Nat`s now set rcgmp's `nonnegative` attribute: both operands
are nonnegative, so immediates untag by a logical shift and divide
unsigned, with no test of the quotient, and every rounding agrees. This
covers `/` and `%` at `Nat` and every division intrinsic at `Nat`
(`edivsi`, `emodsi`, `floordivsi`, `floormodsi`, `divsi_exact` and the
`_lean` ones); `Int` divisions are unchanged. A `Nat` cannot be negative:
no cast from `Int` or a signed integer exists, and `-` on `Nat` truncates
at zero.

`DivisionMode` gains the `nonnegative` field and `rcgmp::divsi_exact` a
`nonnegative` parameter.

AArch64 instructions at -Oaggressive, from the entry to the first `ret`
(the immediate path) and in the whole function:

- `if b == 0 { 0 } else { a / b }` at Nat: 23 to 16, 81 to 74 (`sdiv` to
  `udiv`);
- `divsi_lean` at Nat: 15 to 11, 28 to 24;
- `remsi_lean` at Nat: 13 to 12, 26 to 25;
- `a % ipowi(2, w)` at Nat: 18 to 15, 41 to 38.

Tests: frontend/bigint_nat_division.rr (the -O LLVM IR of a Nat `/` and a
Nat `remsi_lean` divides the untagged immediates unsigned, an Int `/`
signed), the `nonnegative` spelling of the Nat divisions in
frontend/bigint_intrinsics.rr.
